// Payment-processor scenario an LLM might generate without thinking
// about PCI-DSS 3.4 ("PAN must be rendered unreadable in storage and
// in transmission"). Looks like reasonable debugging code; would pass
// every behavior test on benign inputs; ships the cardholder's full
// PAN to the audit log.
//
// Heat refuses at compile time with NL-PCI-3.4: a value tagged
// @cardholder_data flowed into log_info, whose `message` parameter
// requires @audit_safe. The framework-named code (NL-PCI-3.4)
// displaces the generic NL-0500 because the source tag is
// compliance-tagged — auditors and LLMs both see the spec citation.
// The compiler tells the next LLM iteration what's missing — see
// demo_compliance_pci_fixed.heat for the canonical retry: pipe the
// PAN through mask_pan() first.
//
// Frameworks this enforces against:
//   - PCI-DSS 3.4    (PAN rendered unreadable)
//   - GDPR Art. 32   (security of processing personal data)
//   - SOC 2 CC6.7    (transmission/disposal of confidential information)

fn record_payment(card: String @cardholder_data, amount: Int) -> Int [io]:
    // The bug: card is @cardholder_data — Heat refuses to let it
    // reach an audit-log sink without going through mask_pan() or an
    // equivalent sanitizer. Same machinery as @user_input → @sql_safe.
    return log_info(message: card)

fn main() -> Int [io]:
    record_payment(card: "4111111111111111", amount: 4200)
    return 0
